Phase 1: Environment &amp; Scaffolding
 • Initialize GitHub repos with respective licenses (MIT for code, CC BY 4.0 for data).
 • Set up Python environment: Install spaCy, NetworkX, Pandas, and Community-
Louvain.
 • Create &#39;mapping.json&#39;: The Social Schema Registry for resolving aliases (e.g., Partha -&gt;
Arjuna).
Phase 2: Data Engineering &amp; Pre-processing
 • Selection: Download UTF-8 texts from Project Gutenberg (Mahabharata/Hebrew
Bible).
 • Normalization: Write Regex scripts to strip verse markers, metadata, and footnotes.
 • Tokenization: Implement sliding window segmentation (50-100 words).
Phase 3: Entity Extraction &amp; Edge Generation
 • Custom NER: Use spaCy EntityRuler to force recognition of ancient names from a
Master List.
 • Co-occurrence Logic: Create edges based on character proximity within windows.
 • Weighting: Implement edge weights based on frequency of interaction.
Phase 4: Computational Graph Analytics
 • Centrality: Calculate Degree (popularity) and Betweenness (bridges).
 • Topology: Measure Network Density and Diameter.
 • Clustering: Apply Louvain algorithm to detect &#39;houses&#39; or &#39;factions&#39;.
Phase 5: Visualization &amp; DPI Framing

 • Spatial Layout: Use Gephi (ForceAtlas2) to create resume-worthy network
visualizations.
 • DPI Alignment: Frame the Identity Registry (mapping.json) as a parallel to ONDC/UPI
interoperability.
 • Documentation: Create professional READMEs for both repositories.



